ry which underscores the  critical role of collaboration between  experimentalists and theorists if we  really are truly to understand how these  neural circuits underlie behavior  and the long-term goal of the brain  initiative is to illuminate general  principles that can be applied across  the domain of the different behaviors  that are supported by the  by the brain  so just to uh  kind of come back and and and think  about  the topic of today's symposium  uh i want to just briefly summarize  two programs in the brain initiative  that are aimed at understanding neural  circuit function using the new tools  that are being developed by the brain  initiative as well as in adjacent fields  and so the first program i'll highlight  for you here is this  team research-based brain circuits  program or team bcp  and the motivation here is based on the  one of the recommendations from the  brain 2025 report our strategic plan  that was issued in 2014  and the idea here is that rigorous  theory modeling statistics are advancing  our understanding of complex non-linear  brain functions  where human intuition fails so in other  words we need  a strong theoretical basis for asking  good questions for framing our  experiments and for testing our for  testing those experiments again as a  two-way uh iterative process of  experiment validating theory and theory  and forming experiment  now in order to  have a full integration of theory and  experiment  the purpose here of these large  team-based projects  is to incorporate multiple components  that have teams with diverse research  research expertise spanning uh both  multiple experimental disciplines as  well as  theoretical disciplines uh they can  contain up to five synergistic  synergistic research projects and five  research cores and again the theory  model driven approach is central to the  all of these projects that are funded  through the u19 mechanism  and here's just a high level summary of  the u19 projects that are currently  underway uh that are  investigating various aspects of circuit  function underlying behavior uh from  behavior to cognition and memory to  perception  and you can also see some pretty clever  uh acronyms for these projects and  you'll learn uh i think a bit more about  these projects and in the hours to come  so these are these are the large  team-based uh brain circuit program  program projects  we also have  an r01 a series of r01s  in the so-called theories models and  methods portfolio or tmm portfolio so  again the idea here is to emphasize the  importance of theory and driving the  experimentation and also the  experimentation being a check on the  theory  and literally the goal here is to  develop better theories  better models and better methods for  analyzing uh this complex data space for  the ultimate understanding of the  principles that are underlying neural  circuit function  so with that i'm going to hand it over  to our next speaker but before i do that  i need to acknowledge all the great work  that's done by the over 100  nih program staff that are involved in  this effort  and in particular i'd like to call out  one person here muhammad gosemi who was  our next speaker  now muhammad is our nih brain initiative  data scholar uh it's a special uh  program around nih to bring in a data  scholar to look at and to develop a  novel way of  parsing and understanding the data that  we're generating  muhammad is currently an assistant  professor at michigan state university  and he's been with us for the past year  or so as a gator scholar and what  muhammad will tell you about in the next  next uh 15 minutes or so  is this brain works project that he that  he's been working on so brain work  stands for brain initiative workspace to  organize the knowledge space  uh in a nutshell this is to use  data-driven information-based techniques  to mine the literature to mind the  information out there to identify  topic areas that are currently being  supported by brain  and also very importantly to identify  regions of intersection and the idea  here is to uh to discover  in advance what some emerging new areas  of discovery might be based on the work  that's being done out there today so  this is just a very high level overview  in this graph where the nodes represent  research topics supported by brain  and the edges  represent research performed at topic  intersections and you can see here in  these hairball in this one hairball plot  a couple of uh very well represented  nodes for example in magnetic resonance  imaging  in single cell analysis which is at the  core of the brain cell census project i  just mentioned to you of course  optogenetics is a very powerful tool for  understanding uh neural circuit function  neural pathways etc and what we'd like  to do is be able to use this kind and  other sorts of graph analyses to  identify areas of emerging intersections  and ones that might help us predict  where the next big discoveries might  come from so that's just a very high  level overview i'll let muhammad  describe to you the project in much more  detail  and hand it over to him so over to  yamaha hi i'm mohammad kasimi and i'm an  assistant professor of computer science  at michigan state university and this  year it's been my pleasure and  opportunity to serve at the data and  technology advancement national service  scholar  at the nih  and my presentation today is going to be  taking you through brainworks  now brainworks  at its simplest is a  platform with an ambition of converting  scientific papers  into a knowledge graph  the  overall ambition or motivation for this  really contains two pieces  the first is if you think about  scientific disciplines like neuroscience  it's actually composed of many smaller  subdisciplines  so if you look on the left hand side of  the screen there you can see we've got  social genetic  cellular physiological neurological and  behavioral  subdomains of research all of which  together comprise  the domain of neuroscience  and what scientists in these areas are  doing is they're taking uh grants  usually from the government  and  they're doing excellent work and the  consequence of that  that work is  captured within scientific manuscripts  or papers  and our ambition here is to take those  collections of papers across those  varieties of subdomains  and to create a knowledge graph that  captures  all of the major findings embedded  within those papers and across those  papers  now you might naturally wonder well why  should we do something like this aren't  the papers already there can't we just  read them  and the short answer is yeah absolutely  you certainly could read them but the  scientific domain has especially over  the last  20 years or so  grown increasingly complex and the  volume of scientific output has  really been on a pretty steep growth  curve  on the right hand side of the screen  there what i have is the number of  neuroscience related articles  uh that's the y-axis and along the  x-axis i've got the year  and so there's two points i want to draw  your attention to in that graph  if you kind of scroll your eyes to where  that first arrow is  you can see that what that signifies is  if you wanted to kind of keep up with  the literature  around  1980 you'd have to be reading something  on the order of 13 papers a day and  while that's a that's a lot i mean you  could argue that you could maybe you  could if you're really a prodigious  reader you could consume all of those  but if you fast forward to today  you have to read something on the order  of 70 papers per day if you wanted to  just keep up with what's happening in  the scientific community  and it doesn't look like that curve is  going to be heading down or flattening  out anytime soon so  the thought is that  a tool like frameworks  by turning the scientific papers into a  knowledge graph  might allow us to better digest what's  happening within these domains of  science  and choose research directions a little  bit more strategically and and learn  things that otherwise would have been  challenging to observe  now as i mentioned at the start of the  presentation uh  i'm here at the national institutes of  health  as an as a  a data  and technology advancements national  service scholar and my appointment here  has been  uh ongoing for about  eight months now and we'll be continuing  until the end of the year  the project or delivery of this  frameworks platform has happened in five  phases and i'd like to very quickly  highlight what we've already done  and also where we're going in the next  few minutes  so the first thing that we did was  we  had a series of conversations  at the very early days of this project  with experts inside and outside  the nih  several of those experts inside and  outside  are folks who  you will hear from today or  have heard from probably in other  domains i wanted to just  put all those individuals and their  smiling faces here at the beginning to  acknowledge that they were extremely  helpful in helping us craft that vision  that i articulated in the first couple  of slides for this platform  so once we had  completed  that specification of what we wanted the  brainworks platform to do we then moved  to the next very important stage of this  which was  gathering the data that's going to allow  us to build that platform  and  ultimately what we came up with in that  three or four months intensive data  collection period  was is illustrated here on the slide in  front of you starting from the left we  collected information on about 2.7  million  funded nih projects from 1980 to 2020  that collectively account for something  on the order of half a trillion dollars  in investments in scientific communities  and that half a trillion dollars in  investment went to something on the  order of 5 million scientists  embedded at 100 000 institutions  and those 5 million scientists over the  period authored something on the order  of 3.7 million papers  and those papers cited an additional 6  million papers or so to lead us to a  total collection of around 10 million or  9.5 million papers in our collection  now what we did is once we had  identified  all the projects  all of the scientists were involved in  those projects and all the papers that  those scientists had created and we had  added those to our archive we added two  other pieces of information  first that we did was  we assigned topics to each of the papers  so we can take a paper that's published  by some scientists that were funded by  nih and know for example that it was  about neuroplasticity or know that it  was about magnetic resonance imaging or  a set of other topics  we also collected any sort of patents  that were authored  that were influenced or supported in  part by the funds from nih  now with that data collected what we've  been spending the last couple of months  doing  is generating  tools to analyze and visualize this data  set that we have collected  and i'd like to  take you through in the next  10 minutes or so  what some of those preliminary analytic  techniques and visualizations of the  data look like and just note here up  front that  this tool is still evolving so  uh there'll  certainly be more work to come on what  i'm about to show you  so at the end of john's presentation he  showed you something that looked a  little bit like this there was  this is a topics graph  and the way that we generated this was  we took all of those papers that exist  within that 10 million or so paper  collection that we spoke about and we  said hey which subset of those papers  were funded by  uh the brain initiative in from 2014 all  the way up to 2020.  and then let's do two things with that  with those papers let's identify what  the topics are and for each of the  topics let's go ahead and put a node  on this graph that you see in front of  you  and then every time we see  within a given  paper two topics that occur let's draw  an edge between  uh those two topics that we see on this  map here  and so the general structure that you  see in front of you for this topic map  communicates those two pieces of  information what are the sets of topics  that showed up  as well as what are the co-occurrences  between the topics as communicated by  the nodes and the edges respectively  now there's a couple of other pieces of  information that this topic graph  highlights for us the the first is we've  sort of made the text hovering above the  notes larger if that topic was more  represented or showed up  in in the papers more so you can see  magnetic resonance imaging as an example  is a large piece of text single cell  analysis optogenetics and so on  and then if we want to look at an  example a little closer to the top of  the graph we can see that there are  connections between  neural pathways and movement  and so there was there were studies that  have been funded by the brain initiative  at those interfaces now what this graph  really does is it provides a wonderful  overview of the impact that  the nih has had on  a very wide variety of disciplines and  i'm not even showing the whole piece of  it here we sort of simplified it for the  sake of creating a visual  and what i'd like to do is to highlight  what frameworks tool is capable of in  addition to just building a topic graph  i'd like to zoom in on one of the topics  in  this graph specifically the  neuroplasticity topic that you see right  there  and if we take that and we kind of open  it up  we are we might be interested in seeing  of the papers that were published on  neuroplasticity  what are some of the other topics that  co-occurred  with  uh those neuroplasticity papers because  the co-occurrence of the topics could  tell us things about  trends in in research  so for example one of the simplest  analysis we could do is we could  take along the xx here the year  again 1980 to 2020 we could put along  the y-axis the number of papers that  exist in our archive and we could split  that number of papers  according to some of the topical  qualities of these neuroplasticity  papers such as whether they used humans  or animals in the course of study what  you'd see here if you take your eyes and  look from the left all the way to the  right is that  animal studies were predominantly what  were done for neuroplasticity research  in earlier times but as time has gone on  we've seen  more work with humans in this domain and  we have  taken the topics that we've uh imprinted  upon  these neuroscience papers and we've  hierarchically organized them and what  that allows us to do is say if we want  to drill into  a particular topic and kind of explode  it out we have a capability to do that  let's do that for instance with the the  animals topic represented by the orange  bars here we can take that animal's  topic and  spread it out into  various animals that sort of comprise  that that topic  so now what we have is the same plot  that we showed in the last slide  but  in addition to the humans in blue  instead of just having animals we have  the particular kind of animal that was  used in neuroplasticity research at  various points in time  and here what we see is that a slightly  more nuanced story that  yes while animals were used  earlier and you know humans have been  used a lot more in later times  uh  the community switched from using  rats more to using mice more in the  course of experiments in neuroplasticity  now these kinds of conclusions  um are things that maybe a person who's  not an expert at  neuroplasticity or neuroplasticity  research it would take quite a lot of  reading through papers to conclude  something like this and the goal of a  tool like frameworks is to allow for the  exploration of these sorts of  associations  so that more informed research uh  decisions can get made  so if we come back now for a minute to  this plot  it's  i want you to imagine that the ambition  of our tool is that for any topic that  you see here and any topical interface  that you see here  you could zoom in get an idea of how  things are changing over time uh not  only at the level of topics but  in the future at the level of the  natural language text and its properties  as well  but of course  a topic graph or any kind of research  output graph which is what this  represents isn't  something that just exists it's  it's created by  excellent researchers the kind of  researchers that you will be hearing  from in some of the sessions later today  these are world leaders in their domains  and there might be a question of how do  the interactions or collaborations  between researchers in those domains  impact the output of science and the  kinds of things that we end up  discovering  and brainworks also provides a way for  us to understand the role of those  authors in the creation of science  so for example we might  we might be interested in  how collaborative projects impact author  networks as well as their papers and we  might we might have an interest in using  frameworks to answer a question like  that  and so  one thing that i did  uh because we were interested in this  was to take a population of around 1 500  researchers that were affiliated with 60  or so  scientific labs  and to split them into an intervention  and a control group so 30 of those labs  had received an award from the nih to  help foster  additional collaborative efforts across  labs and then 30 of those labs the other  half  had received that same award but at a  later point in time they got it 2019 or  after  so what we did was we said okay let's  take these two groups who we know are  probably similar in terms of their  credentials because they receive the  same collaborative uh award at some  point  let's look at their activities two years  before 2017. that's when the first the  intervention group got it and two years  after which is you know we can we want  to contrast what the output of the two  groups were in these time periods to see  what the impact of this collaborative  award was on output  and  what i'm showing you here in this plot  is a network visualization  that helps us understand some of the  nuances of the answer  what i'd like to do for a minute is  explain first how to interpret the nodes  in the edges and then we're going to  talk a little more formally about what  we concluded from the analysis  so what we have here like any network  plot our nodes and edges the nodes here  correspond to individual authors  sides of the node indicates how much of  an increase in  the author's output there was over the  four year period  and the color of the node indicates  membership in a lab so for example you  can see there's clusters of orange nodes  of green nodes and blue nodes that's  because  those people all work together in a  scientific laboratory  you might also notice that there are  edges and the way to interpret those  edges is that if there's any edge  between authors it means that over that  four year period we were studying there  was an increase in collaboration  you might notice that some of those  edges are orange which means that  there were collaborations across groups  different colored nodes and then some of  the edges are blue which means that they  were collaborations that increased  within the group  okay so what i'm showing you here in  this network is  for that control group the group that  didn't receive the award in the period  that we were thinking about but received  it later this is how their collaborative  network structure looks like  and what you can see here if you kind of  just highlight a couple things is that  there are  several sort of islands that exist that  have highlighted where there was  increased collaboration within the group  but then there were also some  locations here where there were more  collaborations between the groups  at the top we see there's a small  collaboration between three authors from  three labs  there's a major collaboration between  two groups as indicated by that cluster  of green and blue uh interacting  and then there is a little more minor  collaboration between two groups that  i'm showing there at the bottom and this  visualization allows us to sort of see  the data at a level of nuance like that  everything i showed you before  was in a kind of control group that was  the group who didn't receive the award  if we take and do that exact same  analysis  looking at changes in the output for  these  authors over the time period and changes  in their collaborative relationships  over the time period what we see here is  a graph that has some things in common  but then some important differences and  what i'd like to do is highlight first  those differences  you can see here that  in these two locations i've highlighted  there are these big kind of messy super  clusters i'm going to call them with  many different colors and it's sort of a  jumbled up mess  and now while that may not look as  pretty to your eye or to my eyes it's  actually a really wonderful thing uh  from a scientific collaboration  perspective because it means these  authors were  working together so closely that when we  tried to sort of take this network and  and  get people  who were in clusters to be sort of as  separate from each other as we could we  couldn't easily detangle that group when  we were constructing the graph  that bodes extremely well for what the  impact of the collaborative award was in  this case and this is really meant to  highlight  um  that a tool that we're building allows  us to do these kinds of investigations  and to see  nuance in the data that may not have  been easy to do otherwise  so that sort of covers a quick overview  of what we've done with the tool today  and our next steps  what i will be working on between now  and the end of the year  is the development of a user interface  so that instead of  seeing data like this in a presentation  you might be able to click through a  website and play with some of these  graphics yourself do explorations on  your own and see what you come up with  uh specifically i'll be working on  search functionality graph  representation and group comparisons  within the online tool over the coming  months  and so with that i would like to thank  you for your attention  i'd like to really encourage you to  attend and enjoy  the excellent  scientists that we'll be presenting  later today and also  offer for you to reach out and contact  me if you have interest in brainworks or  ideas for what we might be able to do  with the tool thanks
 

